{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"agent_message","text":"{\n  \"A\": \"Research buffered output in src/permutations.cc while initially preserving permutation generation. Remove per-line std::endl flushing and replace per-character formatted insertions with bounded bulk writes. Build a reusable record of length 2*n+4 with fixed delimiters and spaces, fill its symbol positions, and batch records into a reusable output buffer. Compare modest buffer sizes through the unchanged measurement mechanism; handle oversized records without requiring the complete output in memory. Use one consistent output API and flush the final partial buffer. Objective: establish the simplest independently measured reduction in output overhead.\",\n  \"B\": \"Research fused permutation generation and formatting. Maintain the current permutation directly in the symbol positions of a preformatted output record: successor swaps and suffix reversal update only those positions, leaving separators unchanged. This avoids reconstructing every record from a separate string. Compare this approach with a conventional string plus std::next_permutation using an equivalent buffered writer, isolating generation and formatting gains from flushing gains. Preserve native char comparison semantics, duplicate handling, lexicographic order, and safe termination for short or fully non-increasing sequences. Generate from actual input; do not cache complete result sets or require factorial-sized counters.\",\n  \"C\": \"Research ordered parallel generation on the four ARM64 CPU cores, using only threading facilities supported by the unchanged build. Partition the sorted multiset into unique lexicographic prefix subtrees; each task enumerates its remaining suffix in order. Assign tasks lexicographic sequence numbers and use a single ordered writer with bounded buffering and backpressure. Ensure later tasks cannot exhaust buffering resources needed by the earliest unfinished task. Explore coarse task sizes and limited adaptive prefix splitting rather than one task per permutation. Avoid duplicate prefixes, cross-subtree permutations, and overflow-prone factorial ranking. Measure against buffered sequential generation, retaining a general sequential fallback when startup, synchronization, or insufficient independent work outweighs parallelism.\",\n  \"G\": \"Integrate only programs produced by A/B/C, keeping the original and best validated standalone candidates available. Establish A's output changes first, then separately measure combinations with B's fused representation and C's ordered parallelism. Do not assume gains add: copying, queueing, memory traffic, and output serialization can make integration slower. Use the existing correctness and timing procedures without changing tests, oracle, compiler, budgets, target, or permissions. Audit byte-exact formatting, final-buffer draining, task ordering, bounded memory, and empty/single-symbol/duplicate-heavy behavior. Select the fastest valid measured program, including a standalone or sequential candidate if it beats integration.\",\n  \"analysis\": \"The supplied source implements Problem D, All Permutations, Sorted; A/B/C are research roles, not the other contest problems. This run provides only a correct baseline: four correctness cases and mean time 0.133965 seconds, with samples spanning 0.132333–0.134955 seconds. There is no profile or input-size evidence. The strongest source-based hypothesis is output overhead: every permutation uses repeated ostream insertions and an explicit flush. Once buffered, generation and formatting may become relevant; parallelism remains speculative because output must stay ordered and runtime is already short. For nonempty input, each distinct permutation necessarily emits 2*n+4 bytes, limiting algorithmic savings. Every candidate must read through EOF, remove exactly the six whitespace characters recognized by the source, preserve its character ordering, emit each distinct permutation once in lexicographic order as '[ ' followed by each character and a space, then ']\\\\n', and emit nothing for empty filtered input. No optimization may specialize to benchmark values or replace enumeration with expected output.\"\n}"}}
